WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
Avery has six staff hours available for the booking pilot this week.

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:11.925
The example bar divides them into two hours to understand the process, two to prepare a draft, one for training, and one for review.

3
00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:15.743
These are invented numbers, not a recommended staffing formula.

4
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:21.585
The point is that a plan must include time to learn and evaluate, not only time to produce something.

5
00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:27.075
If the draft needs another hour, Avery must adjust the scope, timing, or allocation.

6
00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:32.478
Quietly expecting staff to absorb unlimited extra work would hide the real cost.

7
00:00:32.618 --> 00:00:34.714
Meetings need the same discipline.

8
00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:41.881
Avery can state the decision to be made, invite the people whose knowledge is needed, and finish with clear responsibilities.

9
00:00:42.022 --> 00:00:48.611
A conversation that ends with everyone assuming someone else owns the task has not produced a usable plan.

10
00:00:48.751 --> 00:00:50.519
Here the course functions connect.

11
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.408
Planning sets the service goal.

12
00:00:52.548 --> 00:00:54.444
Organizing assigns the work.

13
00:00:54.584 --> 00:00:57.646
Human resources provides support and development.

14
00:00:57.786 --> 00:00:59.694
Leading builds participation.

15
00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:02.864
Coordinating checks time, resources, and results.

16
00:01:03.004 --> 00:01:10.430
Management brings these functions together in an ordinary service decision, rather than treating them as separate textbook boxes.
